+<header class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">SEMANTIC TWIN LAB / FIELD GUIDE / EVIDENCE EDITION</div><h1>A scene is visible.<br>Its meaning should be<br><em>inspectable.</em></h1><p class="lead">A practical guide to the X3D ontology study and its enhanced local laboratory. Learn how a convincing scene, an RDF graph, a reasoned graph, and a validation report can disagree—and build an evidence trail that makes the disagreement useful.</p><div class="chips"><span class="chip">38 original stations preserved</span><span class="chip">Local computation and recorded evidence separated</span><span class="chip">SPARQL · RDFS · SHACL · X3D</span><span class="chip">Standards checked 14 November 2026</span></div></header>
+<nav aria-label="Contents"><div class="wrap"><a href="#start">Start</a><a href="#contracts">Contracts</a><a href="#reasoning">Reasoning</a><a href="#lists">Lists and identity</a><a href="#evidence">Evidence</a><a href="#repair">Repair</a><a href="#agents">Agents</a><a href="#experiments">Experiments</a><a href="#sources">Sources</a></div></nav>
+<main class="wrap">
+<section class="section" id="start"><div class="k">01 / The useful next step</div><h2>Turn a teaching collection into a repeatable investigation.</h2><p>The supplied lab already links a Cabinet of seven X3D exhibits to RDF facts, ontology classes, editable semantic queries, fault variants, and historical tool receipts. The extension adds an <strong>Evidence Workbench</strong>: a focused place to compare asserted and derived statements, inspect ordered list cells, verify shipped bytes, and understand a narrowly scoped repair.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="k">Explore</span><h3>Open the original lab</h3><p>Use <code>index.html</code> through the local server for the three-strata X3D experience. Follow the 38 stations from facts to the capstone. The lesson numbers and recordings remain part of the supplied study.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">Investigate</span><h3>Open the workbench</h3><p>Use <code>workbench.html</code> for the Reasoning, Ordered lists, Evidence integrity, and Repair witness views. These views use the local semantic stack and do not require WebGL rendering.</p></article></div><pre>python3 serve.py
+
+# Open the local address printed by the server.
+# index.html       original X3D laboratory
+# workbench.html   evidence experiments
+# ONTOLOGY-FIELD-GUIDE.html   this guide</pre><p class="fine">Serve the folder over HTTP. Opening files directly bypasses worker and fetch assumptions. The browser assets are bundled; Node is used by the optional companion and automated semantic tests. The full upstream ontology, source converter, validators, raw session logs, and build environment referenced by historical receipts are not all included.</p><div class="note mint"><p><strong>Central conclusion:</strong> the most valuable advance is a better contract between source, meaning, inference, policy, and evidence. A more elaborate graph is useful only when you can say which statements were observed, which were derived, and which rule accepted the result.</p></div></section>
+<section class="section" id="contracts"><div class="k">02 / Five questions that must stay separate</div><h2>A validation ladder is a set of instruments.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Instrument</th><th>What a pass does not establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Did the XML parse?</td><td>An XML parser, with an explicit entity and DTD policy.</td><td>Correct X3D structure, scene coherence, or a useful image.</td></tr><tr><td>Does the source follow its X3D gate?</td><td>The selected XSD, X3DUOM-informed checks, and semantic diagnostics.</td><td>Task completeness, visual quality, or every intended property.</td></tr><tr><td>What follows from the graph?</td><td>An identified reasoning profile over identified graph inputs.</td><td>Whether the original source was legal or the inferred categories were intended.</td></tr><tr><td>Does this graph satisfy these constraints?</td><td>A shapes graph, an entailment policy, a SHACL engine, and a severity policy.</td><td>A universal correctness verdict independent of those inputs.</td></tr><tr><td>Can we trust this account?</td><td>Available source bytes, hashes, witnesses, receipts, and an authenticated provenance channel where required.</td><td>Authenticity from a hash alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The supplied fault bench is valuable because different layers catch different losses: a missing geometry, a dangling ROUTE, a wrong-type USE, an invalid placement, a forward USE, and a duplicate DEF do not become one problem merely because they all affect the same scene.</p><figure class="flow"><svg viewBox="0 0 1030 148" role="img" aria-label="Source bytes become an asserted graph; a reasoning profile adds a derived layer; a policy evaluates the chosen graph."><defs><marker id="arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" fill="#6c8e98"/></marker></defs><g fill="#102530" stroke="#345460"><rect x="25" y="22" width="210" height="76" rx="5"/><rect x="280" y="22" width="210" height="76" rx="5"/><rect x="535" y="22" width="210" height="76" rx="5"/><rect x="790" y="22" width="210" height="76" rx="5"/></g><g stroke="#6c8e98" marker-end="url(#arrow)"><path d="M239 60h34"/><path d="M494 60h34"/><path d="M749 60h34"/></g><g fill="#e0e9ec" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" font-size="16" text-anchor="middle"><text x="130" y="54">Source bytes</text><text x="385" y="54">Asserted graph</text><text x="640" y="54">Derived layer</text><text x="895" y="54">Policy verdict</text></g><g fill="#9eb1ba" font-family="monospace" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle"><text x="130" y="77">document identity</text><text x="385" y="77">conversion identity</text><text x="640" y="77">rule identity</text><text x="895" y="77">constraint identity</text><text x="385" y="126">Keep source witnesses at each boundary.</text><text x="830" y="126">Keep asserted facts unchanged.</text></g></svg><figcaption>A practical implementation pattern, not a claim that RDF requires this pipeline. The original document remains the witness when a graph projection loses source details.</figcaption></figure></section>
+<section class="section" id="reasoning"><div class="k">03 / The ontology proposes a classification</div><h2>Domain and range infer membership. They do not grant placement permission.</h2><p>Under RDFS, a property use can infer a subject type from its domain and an object type from its range. Several declared domains or ranges apply together. They do not describe a menu of alternatives. <a href="#s-rdfs">[1]</a></p><pre>@prefix ex: &lt;urn:example:&gt; .
+@prefix rdfs: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#&gt; .
+
+ex:slot rdfs:range ex:Child, ex:Visible .
+ex:parent ex:slot ex:box .
+
+# RDFS entails BOTH classifications:
+ex:box a ex:Child, ex:Visible .</pre><p>This tiny example explains the mechanism behind the study's more dramatic over-typing exhibits. It is not evidence that every ontology or every RDFS application is defective. The concrete counts in the study belong to its pinned inputs, rule profile, and receipts.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="k">Question A / Reverse lookup</span><h3>Which ranges already subsume Box?</h3><p>A placement map can compare <code>Box</code>'s existing superclasses against declared property ranges. In the supplied tool findings, <code>X3DNode</code> is the broad named range explaining one such match. The map's exclusions and counting rule matter.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">Question B / Forward inference</span><h3>What type follows after using a property?</h3><p>Given a <code>hasChildren</code> statement, RDFS applies every declared range whether or not the object started as a subtype. The supplied slice includes a child-node range. A reasoner can therefore infer the very type a subsequent class constraint asks for.</p></article></div><p>These are different computations. Replacing the forward-inference explanation with the reverse-map counting result would create a new error while trying to correct an old one.</p><h3>The useful counterfactual</h3><p>Keep the asserted graph fixed. Validate it once without adding range entailments. Then materialise the chosen rules into a separate copy, inspect the added type statements, and run the same constraint again. If a violation disappears without a source edit, the interpretation changed. The source was not repaired.</p><div class="note red"><p><strong>A narrower report is not automatically a better scene.</strong> Inference may satisfy one constraint while expanding the target population of others. Compare individual focus nodes and constraints, not just the final number of findings.</p></div><h3>SHACL has its own contract</h3><p>SHACL validates a data graph against a shapes graph and returns structured results. Record the shape, focus node, path, severity, and message; keep execution failure separate from a completed validation. Also state the application's severity policy. A tool-level success flag is not a clean-scene flag. <a href="#s-shacl">[5]</a></p><pre>{
+  "execution": "completed",
+  "validation": { "conforms": false, "violations": 1 },
+  "evidence": { "inputs_available": true, "recomputed_here": true }
+}</pre><p class="fine">This is a recommended result contract for an integration, not the exact output of every engine in the package.</p></section>
+<section class="section" id="lists"><div class="k">04 / Order and identity are data</div><h2>A colour is a sequence of components.</h2><p>RDF graphs are sets of triples. An ordered collection is represented by a structure such as linked <code>rdf:first</code>/<code>rdf:rest</code> cells; the list's order is in those links, not in Turtle line order. A graph also cannot recover distinctions that its conversion never encoded. <a href="#s-rdf">[2]</a></p><p>The colour-list experiment separates two losses. In the newly checked bundled engine, the plain <code>SELECT ?m ?c</code> list-path query preserves all three occurrences of Pearl's value <code>1</code>; adding <code>DISTINCT</code> collapses them. Neither value-only result supplies an RGB position. Query the cells or explicitly bind each fixed component. An <code>ORDER BY</code> controls a result sequence; alphabetically sorting JSON rows afterwards changes that answer. <a href="#s-sparql">[3]</a></p><pre># A fixed three-component colour, with position carried by the pattern:
+?material x3do:diffuseColor ?head .
+?head rdf:first ?red ; rdf:rest ?cell1 .
+?cell1 rdf:first ?green ; rdf:rest ?cell2 .
+?cell2 rdf:first ?blue ; rdf:rest rdf:nil .</pre><div class="two-terms"><code>(1 1 1)</code><span class="arrow">≠</span><code>{ 1 }</code><span class="fine">DISTINCT can collapse equal values; positions are a separate concern.</span></div><div class="note"><p><strong>A correction worth teaching:</strong> the historical F-08 finding describes the recorded server result. It must not be promoted into a blanket claim that every SPARQL list-path query removes repeated values. The new local regression distinguishes plain SELECT multiplicity from DISTINCT and from missing order.</p></div><h3>Preserve terms before formatting them</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Preserve</th><th>Avoid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unbound variable</td><td>An explicit unbound state, such as JSON <code>null</code> in a display contract.</td><td>An empty string, which is a possible literal value.</td></tr><tr><td>Blank node</td><td>Distinct answer-local labels plus the blank-node term kind.</td><td>One shared <code>_:blank</code> label for every node, or assuming a label is stable across independent parses.</td></tr><tr><td>Typed literal</td><td>Lexical form, datatype IRI, and language when applicable; a numeric convenience value only when safe.</td><td>Converting every number to a JavaScript Number and silently rounding large integers.</td></tr><tr><td>Ordered query</td><td>The engine's result sequence, including multiplicity.</td><td>Sorting for display and then treating the first row as the original query's winner.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Source identity survives where graph identity cannot</h3><div class="note"><strong>Reproduced in this enhancement:</strong> the supplied Cabinet and F6 Turtle graphs have the same bounded graph fingerprint, while the XML audit reports a forward USE only for F6. This is evidence of information lost by that projection. The reports inspect XML and Turtle independently; their derivation correspondence was not rerun. The algorithm is <code>semantic-twin-graph-c14n/v1</code>, not RDFC-1.0. See <code>companion/examples/cabinet-audit.json</code> and <code>fault-F6-audit.json</code>.</div><p>A forward USE and a backward USE can project to the same relationship when source order is omitted. Duplicate DEFs may collapse onto one IRI. A positional IRI can change after an insertion—or survive while naming a different source node. Treat a source locator as a versioned witness: document hash, node selector, source span, and expected original text. Use persistent authoring identifiers where the application needs identity across edits.</p><div class="note"><p><strong>Do not infer source legality from graph coherence.</strong> A projection can erase the evidence needed to reject the original document. Preserve and check both representations.</p></div></section>
+<section class="section" id="evidence"><div class="k">05 / Evidence you can actually inspect</div><h2>A matching hash answers a narrow, useful question.</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning in this edition</th><th>Next useful question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class="status">Computed locally</span></td><td>The current code ran against available inputs and recorded a result.</td><td>Which inputs, profile, engine, and limits produced it?</td></tr><tr><td><span class="status">Verified shipped bytes</span></td><td>Available file bytes match a stated digest.</td><td>Who supplied the digest, and is that channel trusted?</td></tr><tr><td><span class="status gold">Recorded observation</span></td><td>A supplied historical receipt describes a run elsewhere or earlier.</td><td>Are the raw inputs and environment available to reproduce it?</td></tr><tr><td><span class="status gold">Referenced but absent</span></td><td>The receipt names a source, schema, raw log, or build item not in this package.</td><td>What must be supplied before independent verification is possible?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The original manifest contains both a shipped-file digest and a source digest. Some published files were transformed; those two hashes need not be equal. Verifying the shipped copy does not regenerate the missing source or prove the transformation. Preserve both identities and explain the relationship.</p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="k">Source hash</span><h3>Did these exact bytes change?</h3><p>Whitespace, quote style, comments, or attribute order can change this hash even when the RDF graph is isomorphic. This is the right guard for a minimal source edit.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">Graph hash</span><h3>Did this RDF structure change?</h3><p>A declared canonicalisation algorithm can make blank-node relabelling irrelevant. Name the algorithm and its limits. A project-specific bounded algorithm is not automatically interoperable with RDFC-1.0.</p></article></div><p>The W3C RDF Dataset Canonicalization Recommendation defines RDFC-1.0 for canonicalising RDF datasets. Use a conformant implementation and test corpus when interoperable signatures or identifiers depend on that standard. The companion's bounded graph fingerprint is a separately named reference technique. <a href="#s-canon">[4]</a></p><div class="note mint"><p><strong>Integrity is not authenticity.</strong> A hash chain detects edits relative to a trusted root or known head. If an attacker replaces both the content and every expected hash, an unsigned bundle cannot authenticate its original publisher. Keep the chain useful without claiming more.</p></div><h3>A small receipt can still be informative</h3><pre>{
+  "claim": "one selected colour value was replaced",
+  "input_sha256": "…",
+  "output_sha256": "…",
+  "instrument_sha256": "…",
+  "profile": "minimal-attribute-edit",
+  "scope": "source edit only; RDF conversion not rerun"
+}</pre><p>Referencing an instrument dictionary by hash avoids copying the same engine and input metadata into every ledger record. The verifier must also reject missing dictionary entries, modified payloads, reordered records, and an unexpected final head. A valid chain remains an integrity statement, not a claim that the observation itself was true.</p></section>
+<section class="section" id="repair"><div class="k">06 / A repair should have a witness</div><h2>Change the intended bytes. Show the untouched assumptions.</h2><p>The supplied findings show why full-document reserialization can obscure a tiny X3D material edit: it can rewrite quotes and formatting, and dropping a DOCTYPE can change a later conversion pipeline's defaults. A repair demonstration should preserve the document's surrounding bytes and retain an explicit DTD policy.</p><ol class="steps"><li><strong>Bind the edit to a source version.</strong>Require an exact input SHA-256. A selector without a version guard can point at stale text.</li><li><strong>Resolve one unambiguous target.</strong>Identify the element and attribute. Reject duplicate DEF matches, missing fields, or an unexpected old value instead of guessing.</li><li><strong>Replace only the intended attribute value.</strong>Preserve surrounding bytes, quoting, the XML declaration, comments, and a DOCTYPE when present. Do not fetch external entities to perform the edit. A minimal patch cannot recover an already removed declaration or its defaults.</li><li><strong>Emit the source witness.</strong>Show the selector, span or line information, old and new values, changed-byte extent, and before/after hashes.</li><li><strong>Recheck the representations you actually changed.</strong>Parsing, X3D schema validation, semantic diagnostics, graph regeneration, SHACL, and rendering are separate steps. Mark every skipped step.</li></ol><div class="note"><p><strong>The browser F1 demonstration has a deliberate boundary:</strong> its minimal Ruby colour edit is paired with separately evaluated RDF facts for the repair fixture. It is not a full general-purpose XML-to-RDF converter, and it must not be described as a completed source-to-graph regeneration pipeline.</p></div><p>Use the companion's tested local primitives as integration examples. They demonstrate source guards, graph fingerprints, and compact evidence chains; they do not patch the absent upstream <code>x3d_mcp</code> implementation. The original stdio deadlock report still requires a real transport-level regression in that repository.</p></section>
+<section class="section" id="agents"><div class="k">07 / Agent assistance with an accountable boundary</div><h2>Shapes can describe the task without becoming authority.</h2><p>As checked on 14 November 2026, SHACL 1.2 Core is a <strong>W3C Working Draft dated 10 November 2026</strong>. Its <code>sh:agentInstruction</code> property appears among non-validating shape characteristics and carries natural-language guidance. It is not itself an executable constraint, an instruction-priority mechanism, or permission to act. The bundled engines must not be assumed to implement the draft's new features. <a href="#s-shacl12">[6]</a></p><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="k">Agent-facing</span><h3>A concise repair brief</h3><p>Describe the intended outcome, permitted scope, focus node, field, expected old value, and a small candidate change. Carry source and shapes hashes with the brief.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">Machine-facing</span><h3>A repeatable acceptance check</h3><p>Execute named constraints with bounded resources. Return explicit execution, conformance, and evidence statuses. Keep the generated change reviewable before any publication or external write.</p></article></div><p>The recorded sessions show particular tool interactions on particular pinned versions. They are useful case studies. They do not establish that grounding improved an LLM's reliability without a controlled comparison. A strong next study would define a fault corpus, a baseline, task outcomes, failure categories, and the exact evidence supplied to each condition.</p><h3>A contract for a useful semantic tool</h3><ul><li>Preserve RDF term kind, lexical value, datatype, language, unbound state, and result order.</li><li>Separate the identity of source bytes, parsed graph, rule profile, shapes, and result payload.</li><li>Report limits and truncation. A complete-looking page must not silently represent a partial answer.</li><li>Use a killable worker or process for an actual deadline. A timer sharing a blocked execution thread is not a hard timeout.</li><li>Give a source witness and a minimal change proposal. A vague message about a class is not an edit location.</li></ul></section>
+<section class="section" id="experiments"><div class="k">08 / Six experiments worth keeping</div><h2>Predict the outcome before running the instrument.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><span class="k">01 / Beginner</span><h3>Three ones are still three components</h3><p>Inspect a white material as an ordered list. Compare plain SELECT, SELECT DISTINCT, and indexed cells. Confirm where multiplicity survives and why values without indices cannot reconstruct an RGB vector.</p><p class="fine">Evidence: cell order, repeated values, query text, and result terms.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">02 / Intermediate</span><h3>A classification that hides a fault</h3><p>Run the same child-node constraint on asserted data and after range entailment. Find the added type edge that changes the verdict. Do not change the source.</p><p class="fine">Evidence: asserted hash, rules hash, derived delta, and per-constraint result.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">03 / Intermediate</span><h3>Same graph, different text</h3><p>Change Turtle whitespace and blank-node labels in a small fixture. Compare byte hashes with declared graph fingerprints. Then change one literal and repeat.</p><p class="fine">Evidence: different source digests, graph-equivalence result, algorithm name.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">04 / Advanced</span><h3>The ambiguous identity</h3><p>Inspect the supplied forward-USE and duplicate-DEF variants. Identify which source fact the RDF projection omits or merges. Name the additional witness needed.</p><p class="fine">Evidence: source order or uniqueness check beside graph relationships.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">05 / Advanced</span><h3>One attribute, one reviewable patch</h3><p>Use the guarded repair fixture. Try the correct source hash, then a stale one. Confirm the stale edit is rejected. With a synthetic fixture that contains a DOCTYPE, confirm that the edit preserves it; the packaged F1 browser fixture has no DOCTYPE to preserve.</p><p class="fine">Evidence: source guard, target, changed span, and output digest.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="k">06 / Builder</span><h3>A receipt that refuses overclaiming</h3><p>Verify available files, then inspect a historical reference absent from the package. Record the first as a matching local digest and the second as unavailable.</p><p class="fine">Evidence: verified, mismatched, and unavailable are distinct states.</p></article></div><h3>A bounded research programme</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Next implementation</th><th>Completion evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 · Source correspondence</td><td>Carry immutable source versions and precise node witnesses across conversion.</td><td>Inserted siblings and formatting changes do not silently redirect an edit.</td></tr><tr><td>2 · Reasoning provenance</td><td>Keep asserted and derived graphs separate; attach small derivation witnesses.</td><td>Every changed verdict can be tied to a source edit, a rule, or a policy change.</td></tr><tr><td>3 · Semantic interchange</td><td>Adopt a conformant canonicalisation library and a term-safe query-result contract.</td><td>Cross-engine fixtures agree on identity, multiplicity, and ordered answers.</td></tr><tr><td>4 · Transport resilience</td><td>Test the actual stdio MCP session with idle gaps, long-running jobs, and worker failure.</td><td>No leaked child processes, false success flag, or unexplained timeout category.</td></tr><tr><td>5 · Comparative agent study</td><td>Run a small controlled baseline-versus-grounded fault-repair trial.</td><td>Task accuracy, unsupported claims, edit locality, and verification outcomes are reported.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="quote">The strongest semantic twin is one that helps you locate why two answers disagree.</p></section>
+<section class="section" id="sources"><div class="k">09 / Sources and scope</div><h2>Stable standards, a changing draft, and a pinned experiment.</h2><p>Primary standards were checked on 14 November 2026; the cited SHACL 1.2 draft was rechecked on 16 November 2026. These links support the general semantic distinctions above. Concrete X3D counts, original engine timings, and tool-session findings belong to the supplied study and its pinned artifacts; this guide does not extrapolate them to an unseen current upstream repository.</p><ol class="source-list"><li id="s-rdfs"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf-schema/">RDF Schema 1.1</a><span>W3C Recommendation. Sections 3.1 and 3.2 define the range and domain implications, including multiple declarations.</span></li><li id="s-rdf"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/">RDF 1.1 Concepts and Abstract Syntax</a><span>W3C Recommendation. Graphs, terms, graph comparison, and the separation of abstract graphs from concrete documents.</span></li><li id="s-sparql"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/">SPARQL 1.1 Query Language</a><span>W3C Recommendation. Property paths, solution sequences, ordering, projection, and duplicate handling.</span></li><li id="s-canon"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/2024/REC-rdf-canon-20240521/">RDF Dataset Canonicalization</a><span>W3C Recommendation, 21 May 2024. Defines RDFC-1.0. A named project fingerprint is a different contract.</span></li><li id="s-shacl"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/2017/REC-shacl-20170720/">Shapes Constraint Language</a><span>W3C Recommendation, 20 July 2017. Data/shapes graphs, validation, and structured reports.</span></li><li id="s-shacl12"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/WD-shacl12-core-20261110/">SHACL 1.2 Core</a><span>W3C Working Draft, 10 November 2026. Section 6.11.3 contains sh:agentInstruction among non-validating characteristics. Work in progress.</span></li></ol><h3>Project evidence</h3><p class="fine">Read <code>STUDY-AUDIT.md</code> for the source-grounded audit; <code>TOOL-FINDINGS.md</code> when included with the supplied materials for historical tool findings; the package validation notes for checks performed in this enhancement; and the receipt files under <code>data/receipts/</code> for the supplied record. A file mentioned in a receipt is not necessarily present in the archive.</p></section>
